river 0.4.6 / nixpkgs /nix/store/4555ycy58srgn47zrjndmm4hjqd3inxa-river-0.4.6

THIS IS A CEILING, NOT A PIN, AND THE NAME OF THE FILE IS NOW WRONG.  It is
kept because four other files parse its first line, and a rename is not worth
what it would cost them.  What this release name means today: the protocol XML
beside it was copied from river 0.4.6, the bindings were generated from that
XML, and LatticeWM binds every interface at the version that XML declares.  It
does not mean you need river 0.4.6.  Newer is a supported configuration and
always will be — Wayland obliges the compositor to speak the version a client
bound — and the floor is 0.4.5, set by the oldest interface version carrying
every request this program sends.  See src/runtime/session.lisp.

Re-vendoring is therefore how features are picked up, not how installation is
survived.  The paragraphs below are the last time it happened.

Re-vendored from 0.4.5, which is what this directory held until the flake's
build phase first connected to the river the same derivation pins and got a
refusal.  River bumped river_window_manager_v1 from 4 to 5 in a *patch*
release, which is the thing flake.nix's header says is the largest single
threat to this project.  It is worth recording that the refusal was correct
about the fact and wrong about the response: the version had moved, and the
right answer was to keep running, which is what the check does now.

There were two ways out and this is the one taken: move to 0.4.6 rather than
pin river back to 0.4.5.  The argument is the diff, not a preference.  Every
change from 4 to 5 is additive or a clarification of prose:

  river_window_v1.capture_sessions     new event, since 5
  river_output_v1.capture_sessions     new event, since 5
  session_locked                       now also sent in the first manage
                                       sequence if the session is already locked
  min/max size                         constraint relaxed, not tightened
  place_above / place_below            worked examples added, behaviour unchanged
  pointer_warp                         ordering against op_start_pointer stated
  border colour                        RGBA range stated

Nothing was removed and nothing was renamed, so the feared failure — a
pre-release protocol breaking *within* a version number — is not what happened
here.  Pinning back would have frozen the pair on a superseded release that
leaves nixpkgs on its own schedule.

That last sentence used to continue "and the version check refuses in both
directions: a build vendored at 4 cannot start on any river a user installs
today."  That was the reason to change the check rather than a reason to
re-vendor faster.  Refusing upward bought nothing — the diff above is the
evidence, and every line of it is additive — and it cost the session of
everyone whose distribution moved before this project did.  A build vendored
at 4 runs on a river offering 5 now, and receives no capture_sessions.

Both capture_sessions events are now handled.  They were not when this file
was first written, and the note here said so: "a feature we do not have rather
than a protocol we cannot speak".  That was true and it was also the argument
for building the feature, because the one thing you cannot see by looking at
your own screen is that somebody else can see it too.  The count is recorded on
the window and on the output, a change is announced in the echo area, the
shipped status line carries a standing REC for as long as it lasts, and
:CAPTURE-CHANGED is the hook for anything else — src/runtime/capture.lisp.

The other direction is exercised, not assumed.  `make integration' drives a
real headless river, and against Fedora 44's river 0.4.5 — interface version 4,
one below this vendored XML — the whole suite passes: it binds at 4, is never
sent either capture_sessions, and reports that as a version gap rather than a
failure.

AND NOW THE NEAR DIRECTION IS EXERCISED TOO.  This paragraph used to end "both
capture_sessions handlers are therefore covered by the unit suite and by
nothing live, which is honest and is the cost of the range: the only compositor
that can send them is one newer than the oldest we accept."  That was true and
it was a cost worth paying down rather than restating, because a handler no
compositor has ever driven is a handler that has never been told it is wrong.

The suite has since been run against river 0.4.6 as well — interface version 5,
which is what the XML in this directory was generated from — and both events
arrive: a count on the output, a count on the window, and :CAPTURE-CHANGED
firing with the two arguments it declares.  Nothing is reported as a version
gap in that run, because there is no gap to report.

So the range is now checked at both ends by the same suite, and the two runs
assert different things.  The floor run says a request this program sends is
one an older river understands.  The ceiling run says an event this program
binds for is one it can actually decode.  Only the first of those was ever in
doubt out loud; the second is the one that would fail silently.

The three input protocols moved 1 -> 2 in the same release, each adding a
`done' event this program does not listen for.  They used to bind at whatever
version river offered, which this file described as degrading "to their old
behaviour against an older river instead of refusing."  True, and only half the
axis: against a *newer* river the same line promised to decode events these
bindings have never seen, and a promise like that is not broken loudly.  All
six protocols have a ceiling constant now and all six are clamped to it; gate 5
checks every one against the XML here rather than two of them.

wayland.xml is wayland 1.23, taken from wayflan's own vendored copy.  It is
here for gate 8 and for nothing else: wayflan generates the core protocol into
its own package, so nothing under src/protocol/ compiles this file.

Vendoring it is what makes gate 8 total rather than partial.  The gate checks
that every event we listen for exists on the interface we listen for it on, by
reading the XML in this directory — so an interface whose XML is absent is a
handler the gate cannot see.  We listen to wl_output for its name and its scale
factor, because river_output_v1 carries neither, and without this file that was
exactly the shape of handler gate 8 was written to catch.
